{"task_id": "HumanEval/122", "solution": "```python\n# src/__init__.py\n# Initializes the src package\n\n\n# src/add_elements_service.py\nfrom src.utils import Utils\n\nclass AddElementsService:\n    \"\"\"\n    Encapsulates the core logic for calculating the sum of qualifying elements.\n    It is a stateless service class providing the main business logic.\n    \"\"\"\n    @staticmethod\n    def add_elements(arr: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Calculates the sum of elements with at most two digits from the first k elements of arr.\n        This method iterates through the specified prefix, adding qualifying numbers to a total.\n        \"\"\"\n        total_sum = 0\n        # Iterate through the first 'k' elements, ensuring index does not exceed array bounds.\n        for i in range(min(k, len(arr))):\n            if Utils.is_two_digit(arr[i]):\n                total_sum += arr[i]\n        return total_sum\n\n\n# src/utils.py\nclass Utils:\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides general utility functions, specifically for checking properties of numbers.\n    This class is stateless and offers static methods for common checks.\n    \"\"\"\n    @staticmethod\n    def is_two_digit(num: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Checks if an integer has at most two digits (absolute value <= 99).\n        This includes single-digit numbers and negative two-digit numbers.\n        \"\"\"\n        return -99 <= num <= 99\n\n\n# pyproject.toml\n[project]\nname = \"sum-calculator\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"A utility to calculate a specific sum from a given array of integers.\"\nreadme = \"README.md\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.8\"\ndependencies = []\n\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"setuptools>=61.0\"]\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n\n[tool.setuptools.packages.find]\nwhere = [\"src\"]\n\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/123", "solution": "```python\nclass CollatzCalculator:\n    def _generate_sequence(self, n: int) -> list[int]:\n        \"\"\"Generates the complete Collatz sequence for a given positive integer n.\n        Raises ValueError if n is not a positive integer.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(n, int) or n <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a positive integer.\")\n        \n        sequence = [n]\n        current = n\n        while current != 1:\n            if current % 2 == 0:\n                current //= 2\n            else:\n                current = (3 * current) + 1\n            sequence.append(current)\n        return sequence\n\n    def _filter_and_sort_odd(self, sequence: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n        \"\"\"Filters a sequence to extract unique odd numbers and returns them in sorted order.\"\"\"\n        odd_numbers = sorted(list(set(num for num in sequence if num % 2 != 0)))\n        return odd_numbers\n\n    def get_odd_collatz(self, n: int) -> list[int]:\n        \"\"\"Calculates the Collatz sequence for 'n' and returns a sorted list of unique odd numbers found within it.\n        Propagates ValueError from _generate_sequence for invalid inputs.\"\"\"\n        sequence = self._generate_sequence(n)\n        return self._filter_and_sort_odd(sequence)\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/122", "solution": "```\n# pyproject.toml\n[project]\nname = \"sum_utility\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"A utility to calculate a specific sum from an array of integers.\"\nauthors = [{ name = \"Your Name\", email = \"your.email@example.com\" }]\ndependencies = []\n\n[project.optional-dependencies]\ndev = [\"pytest>=7.0.0\"]\n\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"setuptools>=61.0\"]\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n\n```\n# README.md\n# Sum Utility Project\n\nThis project provides a Python utility function `add_elements` that calculates a specific sum from an array of integers. The sum is based on considering only elements with at most two digits from the first `k` elements of the array.\n\n## Features\n\n-   Sums elements from the beginning of an array.\n-   Filters elements based on having at most two digits (i.e., -99 to 99, inclusive).\n\n## File Structure\n\n```\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 src/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 __init__.py         # Package initializer\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 utils.py            # Core utility function: add_elements\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 main.py             # Demonstration script\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 tests/\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 test_utils.py       # Unit tests for add_elements\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 pyproject.toml          # Project metadata and dependencies\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 README.md               # Project description\n```\n\n## Setup and Installation\n\n1.  **Clone the repository:**\n    ```bash\n    git clone <repository_url>\n    cd sum_utility_project\n    ```\n\n2.  **Create a virtual environment (recommended):**\n    ```bash\n    python -m venv .venv\n    source .venv/bin/activate  # On Windows: .venv\\Scripts\\activate\n    ```\n\n3.  **Install dependencies:**\n    ```bash\n    pip install .\n    pip install \".[dev]\" # For installing pytest\n    ```\n\n## Usage\n\nTo run the demonstration script:\n\n```bash\npython src/main.py\n```\n\nThis will execute `main.py`, which calls `add_elements` with example inputs and prints the results.\n\n## Running Tests\n\nTo run the unit tests:\n\n```bash\npytest tests/\n```\n\nThis will execute the tests defined in `tests/test_utils.py` and report the results.\n\n```\n# src/__init__.py\n# Package initializer for 'src'\n\n```\n# src/utils.py\ndef add_elements(arr: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of integers from the first k elements of arr\n    that have at most two digits (between -99 and 99 inclusive).\n    \"\"\"\n    total_sum = 0\n    for i in range(k):\n        element = arr[i]\n        # Checks if the element is within the two-digit range.\n        if -99 <= element <= 99:\n            total_sum += element\n    return total_sum\n\n```\n# src/main.py\nfrom src.utils import add_elements\n\ndef main():\n    \"\"\"\n    Demonstrates the add_elements function with various example inputs.\n    Prints the inputs and the calculated sums.\n    \"\"\"\n    print(\"--- Demonstration of add_elements ---\")\n\n    # Example 1: Basic scenario with mixed qualifying and non-qualifying numbers.\n    arr1 = [111, 21, 3, 4000, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9]\n    k1 = 4\n    result1 = add_elements(arr1, k1)\n    print(f\"Input: arr = {arr1}, k = {k1}\")\n    print(f\"Output: {result1}\") # Expected: 24 (21 + 3)\n    print(\"-\" * 30)\n\n    # Example 2: Scenario where no elements qualify.\n    arr2 = [100, 200, 300]\n    k2 = 3\n    result2 = add_elements(arr2, k2)\n    print(f\"Input: arr = {arr2}, k = {k2}\")\n    print(f\"Output: {result2}\") # Expected: 0\n    print(\"-\" * 30)\n\n    # Example 3: Scenario with negative qualifying numbers.\n    arr3 = [-10, 5, -99, 1000]\n    k3 = 3\n    result3 = add_elements(arr3, k3)\n    print(f\"Input: arr = {arr3}, k = {k3}\")\n    print(f\"Output: {result3}\") # Expected: -10 + 5 + -99 = -104\n    print(\"-\" * 30)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n\n```\n# tests/test_utils.py\nimport pytest\nfrom src.utils import add_elements\n\nclass TestUtils:\n    \"\"\"\n    Contains unit tests for the add_elements function.\n    Each test method verifies a specific scenario or edge case.\n    \"\"\"\n    def test_add_elements_basic(self):\n        arr = [111, 21, 3, 4000, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9]\n        k = 4\n        assert add_elements(arr, k) == 24\n\n    def test_add_elements_no_two_digit(self):\n        arr = [100, 200, 300, 400]\n        k = 4\n        assert add_elements(arr, k) == 0\n\n    def test_add_elements_all_two_digit(self):\n        arr = [10, 20, 30, 40]\n        k = 4\n        assert add_elements(arr, k) == 100\n\n    def test_add_elements_negative_numbers(self):\n        arr = [-10, -5, 100, 20]\n        k = 3\n        assert add_elements(arr, k) == -15\n\n    def test_add_elements_edge_k_min(self):\n        arr = [50, 100, 200]\n        k = 1\n        assert add_elements(arr, k) == 50\n\n    def test_add_elements_edge_k_max(self):\n        arr = [1, 2, 100]\n        k = 3\n        assert add_elements(arr, k) == 3\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/115", "solution": "import math\n\nclass WellFiller:\n    \"\"\"\n    A utility class for calculating bucket operations to empty wells.\n    This class provides a static method to perform the calculation.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def max_fill(grid: list[list[int]], capacity: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Calculates the total number of times buckets need to be lowered to empty all wells.\n        It sums water units per well and applies ceiling division for operations.\n        \"\"\"\n        total_lowers = 0\n        for well in grid:\n            water_units = sum(well)\n            if water_units > 0:\n                operations_for_well = math.ceil(water_units / capacity)\n                total_lowers += operations_for_well\n        return total_lowers"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/123", "solution": "```python\n# src/collatz_calculator/__init__.py\n# This file initializes the collatz_calculator package.\n\n```\n```python\n# src/collatz_calculator/models.py\nclass CollatzSequence:\n    \"\"\"Represents a Collatz sequence as a list of integers.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, sequence: list[int]):\n        self.sequence = sequence\n\n```\n```python\n# src/collatz_calculator/utils.py\nclass CollatzUtils:\n    \"\"\"Utility class for common number checks.\"\"\"\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def is_odd(num: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if a number is odd.\"\"\"\n        return num % 2 != 0\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def is_even(num: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if a number is even.\"\"\"\n        return num % 2 == 0\n\n```\n```python\n# src/collatz_calculator/services.py\nfrom .models import CollatzSequence\nfrom .utils import CollatzUtils\n\nclass CollatzService:\n    \"\"\"Service for generating Collatz sequences and extracting odd numbers.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, utils: CollatzUtils):\n        self._utils = utils\n\n    def _validate_input(self, n: int) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Validates if n is a positive integer.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(n, int) or n <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a positive integer.\")\n\n    def generate_sequence(self, n: int) -> CollatzSequence:\n        \"\"\"Generates the full Collatz sequence for n.\"\"\"\n        self._validate_input(n)\n        sequence = [n]\n        current_term = n\n        while current_term != 1:\n            if self._utils.is_even(current_term):\n                current_term //= 2\n            else:\n                current_term = 3 * current_term + 1\n            sequence.append(current_term)\n        return CollatzSequence(sequence)\n\n    def get_odd_collatz(self, n: int) -> list[int]:\n        \"\"\"Calculates and returns a sorted list of unique odd numbers in the Collatz sequence for n.\"\"\"\n        collatz_sequence_obj = self.generate_sequence(n)\n        odd_numbers = sorted(list(set(\n            term for term in collatz_sequence_obj.sequence if self._utils.is_odd(term)\n        )))\n        return odd_numbers\n\n```\n```python\n# src/collatz_calculator/main.py\nfrom .services import CollatzService\nfrom .utils import CollatzUtils\n\n# Initialize dependencies for the main entry point.\n_collatz_utils = CollatzUtils()\n_collatz_service = CollatzService(utils=_collatz_utils)\n\ndef get_odd_collatz(n: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer n, return a sorted list of unique odd numbers in its Collatz sequence.\n    For example: get_odd_collatz(5) returns [1, 5].\n    \"\"\"\n    return _collatz_service.get_odd_collatz(n)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    # Example usage based on the design specification.\n    try:\n        result = get_odd_collatz(5)\n        print(f\"get_odd_collatz(5) returns {result}\")\n\n        result = get_odd_collatz(1)\n        print(f\"get_odd_collatz(1) returns {result}\")\n\n        result = get_odd_collatz(10)\n        print(f\"get_odd_collatz(10) returns {result}\")\n\n        # This call demonstrates error handling for invalid input.\n        # get_odd_collatz(0)\n    except ValueError as e:\n        print(f\"Error: {e}\")\n\n```\n```toml\n# src/pyproject.toml\n[project]\nname = \"collatz_calculator\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"A utility to calculate odd numbers in a Collatz sequence.\"\nauthors = [\n    { name = \"Your Name\", email = \"your.email@example.com\" },\n]\ndependencies = [] # No runtime dependencies beyond standard library\n\n[project.optional-dependencies]\ndev = [\n    \"pytest>=7.0.0\",\n]\n\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"setuptools>=61.0\"]\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n\n[tool.pytest.ini_options]\npythonpath = \"src\"\n```\n```markdown\n# src/README.md\n# Collatz Calculator\n\nThis project provides a Python utility to calculate all unique odd numbers within a Collatz sequence for a given positive integer.\n\n## Features\n\n- Generates a Collatz sequence for a positive integer `n`.\n- Identifies and returns a sorted list of all unique odd numbers in the sequence.\n- Handles input validation for `n`.\n\n## Usage\n\nThe main entry point is the `get_odd_collatz` function located in `src/collatz_calculator/main.py`.\n\n### Example\n\n```python\nfrom collatz_calculator.main import get_odd_collatz\n\n# Calculate odd numbers in the Collatz sequence for 5\nresult = get_odd_collatz(5)\nprint(f\"Odd numbers for n=5: {result}\")  # Expected: [1, 5]\n\n# Calculate for 10\nresult = get_odd_collatz(10)\nprint(f\"Odd numbers for n=10: {result}\") # Expected: [1, 5]\n\n# Handles the base case n=1\nresult = get_odd_collatz(1)\nprint(f\"Odd numbers for n=1: {result}\")  # Expected: [1]\n\n# Invalid input raises a ValueError\ntry:\n    get_odd_collatz(0)\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print(f\"Error: {e}\") # Expected: Error: Input 'n' must be a positive integer.\n```\n\n## Development\n\n### Setup\n\n1.  **Clone the repository:**\n    ```bash\n    git clone <repository-url>\n    cd collatz-calculator\n    ```\n2.  **Create a virtual environment (recommended):**\n    ```bash\n    python -m venv venv\n    source venv/bin/activate  # On Windows: `venv\\Scripts\\activate`\n    ```\n3.  **Install development dependencies:**\n    ```bash\n    pip install -e \".[dev]\"\n    ```\n\n### Running Tests\n\nUnit tests are located in the `tests/` directory.\n\n```bash\npytest\n```\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/124", "solution": "```python\nimport os\n\n# Create the directory structure if it doesn't exist\nos.makedirs(\"src/date_validator\", exist_ok=True)\n\n# Write to src/date_validator/constants.py\nwith open(\"src/date_validator/constants.py\", \"w\") as f:\n    f.write(\"\"\"from typing import Dict\n\nDAYS_IN_MONTH_MAP: Dict[int, int] = {\n    1: 31,  # January has 31 days.\n    2: 29,  # February has a maximum of 29 days as per the design.\n    3: 31,  # March has 31 days.\n    4: 30,  # April has 30 days.\n    5: 31,  # May has 31 days.\n    6: 30,  # June has 30 days.\n    7: 31,  # July has 31 days.\n    8: 31,  # August has 31 days.\n    9: 30,  # September has 30 days.\n    10: 31, # October has 31 days.\n    11: 30, # November has 30 days.\n    12: 31  # December has 31 days.\n}\n\"\"\")\n\n# Write to src/date_validator/__init__.py\nwith open(\"src/date_validator/__init__.py\", \"w\") as f:\n    f.write(\"\") # Empty init file for package\n\n# Write to src/date_validator/validator.py\nwith open(\"src/date_validator/validator.py\", \"w\") as f:\n    f.write(\"\"\"from src.date_validator.constants import DAYS_IN_MONTH_MAP\n\nclass DateValidator:\n    @staticmethod\n    def valid_date(date_str: str) -> bool:\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"\n        Validates if a date string adheres to \"mm-dd-yyyy\" format and represents a valid calendar date.\n        It orchestrates all internal validation steps.\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"\n        if not DateValidator._is_non_empty(date_str):\n            return False\n\n        is_valid_format, month, day, year = DateValidator._is_format_valid(date_str)\n        if not is_valid_format:\n            return False\n\n        if not DateValidator._is_month_valid(month):\n            return False\n\n        if not DateValidator._is_day_valid(day, month):\n            return False\n\n        return True\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _is_non_empty(date_str: str) -> bool:\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"Checks if the input date string is not empty.\\\"\\\"\\\"\n        return bool(date_str.strip())\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _is_format_valid(date_str: str) -> tuple[bool, int, int, int]:\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"\n        Checks if the date string matches \"mm-dd-yyyy\" format and attempts to parse components.\n        Returns validity and parsed month, day, year (or 0s if invalid).\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"\n        parts = date_str.split('-')\n        if len(parts) != 3:\n            return False, 0, 0, 0\n        \n        try:\n            month = int(parts[0])\n            day = int(parts[1])\n            year = int(parts[2])\n            return True, month, day, year\n        except ValueError:\n            return False, 0, 0, 0\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _is_month_valid(month: int) -> bool:\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"Checks if the month integer is within the valid range [1, 12].\\\"\\\"\\\"\n        return 1 <= month <= 12\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _is_day_valid(day: int, month: int) -> bool:\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"\n        Checks if the day integer is valid for the given month using predefined day counts.\n        It depends on the DAYS_IN_MONTH_MAP for month-specific day limits.\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"\n        if not (1 <= day):\n            return False\n        \n        max_days = DAYS_IN_MONTH_MAP.get(month)\n        if max_days is None: # This case should ideally not be reached if _is_month_valid passed.\n            return False\n        \n        return day <= max_days\n\"\"\")\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/125", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Union\n\nclass TextSplitterService:\n    @staticmethod\n    def split_words(txt: str) -> Union[List[str], int]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Processes a string according to specific delimiter rules or character properties.\n        Returns a list of strings if splitting, or an integer count.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not txt:\n            return 0 # An empty string results in 0 for the counting scenario.\n\n        if ' ' in txt:\n            return txt.split(' ')\n        elif ',' in txt:\n            return txt.split(',')\n        else:\n            count = 0\n            for char in txt:\n                if 'a' <= char <= 'z':\n                    alphabetical_order = ord(char) - ord('a')\n                    if alphabetical_order % 2 != 0:\n                        count += 1\n            return count\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/125", "solution": "# src/utils.py\ndef _is_odd_order_lowercase_letter(char: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a given character is a lowercase letter with an odd alphabetical order (0-indexed).\n    This function returns true if the character is 'b', 'd', 'f', etc.\n    \"\"\"\n    if 'a' <= char <= 'z':\n        return (ord(char) - ord('a')) % 2 != 0\n    return False\n\n# src/text_processor.py\nfrom typing import List, Union\nfrom src.utils import _is_odd_order_lowercase_letter\n\ndef split_words(txt: str) -> Union[List[str], int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Processes text by splitting on whitespace, then commas, or counting specific characters.\n    It applies rules hierarchically: whitespace first, then commas, then odd-ordered lowercase letter count.\n    \"\"\"\n    if ' ' in txt:\n        return txt.split(' ')\n    elif ',' in txt:\n        return txt.split(',')\n    else:\n        count = 0\n        for char in txt:\n            if _is_odd_order_lowercase_letter(char):\n                count += 1\n        return count"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/124", "solution": "```python\n# src/date_validator/__init__.py\n# Package initialization for date_validator.\n\n# This file can be empty or contain package-level exports.\n```\n```python\n# src/date_validator/constants.py\n\n# Stores immutable configuration data related to date validation.\nDAYS_IN_MONTH = {\n    1: 31, 3: 31, 5: 31, 7: 31, 8: 31, 10: 31, 12: 31,\n    4: 30, 6: 30, 9: 30, 11: 30,\n    2: 29  # Max 29 days for February as per requirement\n}\n\nMONTHS_31_DAYS = {1, 3, 5, 7, 8, 10, 12}\nMONTHS_30_DAYS = {4, 6, 9, 11}\nMONTHS_29_DAYS = {2}\n```\n```python\n# src/date_validator/exceptions.py\n\n# Custom exceptions for date validation issues.\nclass InvalidDateFormatError(ValueError):\n    \"\"\"Exception raised for errors in the date string format.\"\"\"\n    pass\n\nclass InvalidDateValueError(ValueError):\n    \"\"\"Exception raised for errors in the numeric values of date components.\"\"\"\n    pass\n```\n```python\n# src/date_validator/validator.py\nfrom src.date_validator.constants import DAYS_IN_MONTH\nfrom src.date_validator.exceptions import InvalidDateFormatError, InvalidDateValueError\n\nclass DateValidator:\n    \"\"\"Encapsulates business logic for validating a date string.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._days_in_month = DAYS_IN_MONTH\n\n    def is_valid(self, date_str: str) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"The main public method to validate a date string.\"\"\"\n        if not date_str:\n            return False\n\n        try:\n            month, day, year = self._parse_date(date_str)\n            if not self._is_month_valid(month):\n                return False\n            if not self._is_day_valid(day, month):\n                return False\n        except (InvalidDateFormatError, InvalidDateValueError):\n            return False\n        except Exception: # Catch any unexpected errors during parsing/validation\n            return False\n\n        return True\n\n    def _parse_date(self, date_str: str) -> tuple[int, int, int]:\n        \"\"\"Parses the mm-dd-yyyy formatted string into integer month, day, and year.\"\"\"\n        parts = date_str.split('-')\n        if len(parts) != 3:\n            raise InvalidDateFormatError(f\"Date string '{date_str}' does not match mm-dd-yyyy format.\")\n\n        try:\n            month = int(parts[0])\n            day = int(parts[1])\n            year = int(parts[2])\n        except ValueError:\n            raise InvalidDateValueError(f\"Date components in '{date_str}' are not valid integers.\")\n\n        return month, day, year\n\n    def _is_month_valid(self, month: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if the month is within the valid range [1, 12].\"\"\"\n        return 1 <= month <= 12\n\n    def _is_day_valid(self, day: int, month: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if the day is valid for the given month.\"\"\"\n        if not (1 <= day):\n            return False\n        max_days = self._days_in_month.get(month)\n        if max_days is None: # Should not happen if month is already validated\n            return False\n        return day <= max_days\n\n_date_validator_instance = DateValidator()\n\ndef valid_date(date_str: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Validates a given date string according to predefined rules.\n    This function delegates the validation to an internal DateValidator instance.\n    \"\"\"\n    return _date_validator_instance.is_valid(date_str)\n```\n```python\n# main.py\nfrom src.date_validator.validator import valid_date\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    # Example usage of the valid_date function.\n    print(f\"\\'03-11-2000\\' is valid: {valid_date('03-11-2000')}\")\n    print(f\"\\'15-01-2012\\' is valid: {valid_date('15-01-2012')}\")\n    print(f\"\\'04-0-2040\\' is valid: {valid_date('04-0-2040')}\")\n    print(f\"\\'06-04-2020\\' is valid: {valid_date('06-04-2020')}\")\n    print(f\"\\'06/04/2020\\' is valid: {valid_date('06/04/2020')}\")\n    print(f\"\\'\\' is valid: {valid_date('')}\")\n    print(f\"\\'02-29-2020\\' is valid: {valid_date('02-29-2020')}\")\n    print(f\"\\'02-30-2020\\' is valid: {valid_date('02-30-2020')}\")\n    print(f\"\\'aa-bb-cccc\\' is valid: {valid_date('aa-bb-cccc')}\")\n    print(f\"None is valid: {valid_date(None)}\")\n```"}
