{"task_id": "HumanEval/132", "solution": "```python\n# src/bracket_validator/__init__.py\n# Intentionally left empty to mark bracket_validator as a Python package.\n```\n```python\n# src/bracket_validator/exceptions.py\nclass InvalidInputError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Custom exception raised for invalid input characters in bracket strings.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, message=\"Input string contains invalid characters. Only '[' and ']' are allowed.\"):\n        self.message = message\n        super().__init__(self.message)\n```\n```python\n# src/bracket_validator/validator.py\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidInputError\n\nclass BracketValidator:\n    \"\"\"Provides utility methods to validate and analyze bracket strings.\"\"\"\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _validate_input(bracket_string: str) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Ensures the input is a string containing only square bracket characters.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(bracket_string, str):\n            raise TypeError(\"Input must be a string.\")\n        for char in bracket_string:\n            if char not in ['[', ']']:\n                raise InvalidInputError()\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def is_nested(bracket_string: str) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Checks if the given string of square brackets contains at least one valid nested bracket subsequence.\n        A nested subsequence implies an inner valid bracket pair enclosed by an outer bracket.\n        \"\"\"\n        BracketValidator._validate_input(bracket_string)\n\n        stack = []\n        for char in bracket_string:\n            if char == '[':\n                stack.append(char)\n            elif char == ']':\n                if not stack:\n                    # Closing bracket without an open one does not form a valid pair for nesting.\n                    continue\n                stack.pop()\n                if stack:\n                    # If stack is not empty after popping, it means the just-closed pair was nested.\n                    return True\n        return False\n```\n```toml\n# pyproject.toml\n[project]\nname = \"bracket-validator\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"A utility to determine if a string of square brackets contains at least one valid nested bracket subsequence.\"\nreadme = \"README.md\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.8\"\nlicense = { text = \"MIT\" }\nkeywords = [\"brackets\", \"validation\", \"nesting\"]\nauthors = [\n  { name = \"Your Name\", email = \"your.email@example.com\" },\n]\nclassifiers = [\n    \"Programming Language :: Python :: 3\",\n    \"License :: OSI Approved :: MIT License\",\n    \"Operating System :: OS Independent\",\n]\n\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"setuptools>=61.0\"]\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n\n[project.urls]\nHomepage = \"https://github.com/yourusername/bracket-validator\"\n\"Bug Tracker\" = \"https://github.com/yourusername/bracket-validator/issues\"\n```\n```markdown\n# README.md\n\n# Bracket Validator\n\nA Python utility to determine if a given string of square brackets contains at least one valid nested bracket subsequence.\n\n## Features\n\n-   **Bracket Nesting Validation:** Efficiently checks if a string composed solely of `[` and `]` characters contains any nested bracket structures (e.g., `[[]]`, `[[][]]`).\n-   **Input Validation:** Ensures that the input is a string and contains only allowed bracket characters, raising specific errors for invalid inputs.\n\n## Installation\n\nThis package is intended to be used as a library. You can include it directly in your project or integrate it into your build system.\n\n## Usage\n\nTo use the `BracketValidator`, simply import the `BracketValidator` class and call its `is_nested` static method:\n\n```python\nfrom src.bracket_validator.validator import BracketValidator\nfrom src.bracket_validator.exceptions import InvalidInputError\n\n# Valid nested cases\nprint(f\"'[[]]' is nested: {BracketValidator.is_nested('[[]]')}\")         # True\nprint(f\"'[[][]]' is nested: {BracketValidator.is_nested('[[][]]')}\")     # True\n\n# Valid non-nested cases\nprint(f\"'[]' is nested: {BracketValidator.is_nested('[]')}\")             # False\nprint(f\"'[][]]' is nested: {BracketValidator.is_nested('[][]]')}\")       # False\n\n# Invalid input handling\ntry:\n    BracketValidator.is_nested('[a]')\nexcept InvalidInputError as e:\n    print(f\"Error: {e}\")\n\ntry:\n    BracketValidator.is_nested(123)\nexcept TypeError as e:\n    print(f\"Error: {e}\")\n```\n\n## API\n\n### `BracketValidator.is_nested(bracket_string: str) -> bool`\n\nThe main function to check for nested bracket subsequences.\n\n-   **Parameters:**\n    -   `bracket_string` (str): The string composed of `[` and `]` characters to be validated.\n-   **Returns:**\n    -   `bool`: `True` if a nested subsequence is found, `False` otherwise.\n-   **Raises:**\n    -   `TypeError`: If the input `bracket_string` is not a string.\n    -   `InvalidInputError`: If `bracket_string` contains characters other than `[` or `]` .\n\n## Development\n\n### Running Tests\n\nThis project uses `unittest` for testing. To run the tests, navigate to the project root and execute:\n\n```bash\npython -m unittest discover tests\n```\n\n### Project Structure\n\n```\n.\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 src/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 bracket_validator/\n\u2502   \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 __init__.py\n\u2502   \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 validator.py\n\u2502   \u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 exceptions.py\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 tests/\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 test_validator.py\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 pyproject.toml\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 README.md\n```\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/132", "solution": "```python\ndef is_nested(string):\n    '''\n    Create a function that takes a string as input which contains only square brackets.\n    The function should return True if and only if there is a valid subsequence of brackets \n    where at least one bracket in the subsequence is nested.\n    '''\n    open_count = 0\n    has_nested_structure = False\n    for char in string:\n        if char == '[':\n            open_count += 1\n        elif char == ']':\n            if open_count > 0:\n                if open_count >= 2:\n                    has_nested_structure = True\n                open_count -= 1\n            else:\n                # Unmatched closing bracket, invalidates potential nesting.\n                return False\n        \n    return has_nested_structure\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/133", "solution": "```python\n# src/__init__.py\n# Initializes the 'src' package.\n```\n```python\n# src/utils.py\nimport math\n\nclass MathUtils:\n    @staticmethod\n    def ceil_round(number: float | int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Rounds a number up to the nearest integer (ceiling).\n        This function uses math.ceil to perform the rounding.\n        \"\"\"\n        return math.ceil(number)\n```\n```python\n# src/calculator.py\nfrom src.utils import MathUtils\n\nclass SumSquaresCalculator:\n    def calculate(self, numbers: list[float | int]) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Calculates the sum of squared numbers in the given list,\n        rounding each element to the upper integer (Ceiling) first.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(numbers, list):\n            raise TypeError(\"Input must be a list.\")\n        \n        if not numbers:\n            return 0\n\n        rounded_and_squared_numbers = []\n        for num in numbers:\n            if not isinstance(num, (int, float)):\n                raise TypeError(f\"List elements must be numbers, got {type(num)}\")\n            rounded_num = MathUtils.ceil_round(num)\n            squared_num = rounded_num * rounded_num\n            rounded_and_squared_numbers.append(squared_num)\n        \n        return sum(rounded_and_squared_numbers)\n\ndef sum_squares(lst: list[float | int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Convenience function to calculate the sum of squared numbers.\n    It instantiates SumSquaresCalculator and delegates the calculation.\n    \"\"\"\n    calculator = SumSquaresCalculator()\n    return calculator.calculate(lst)\n```\n```toml\n# pyproject.toml\n[project]\nname = \"sum-squares-calculator\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"A utility to calculate the sum of squares of ceiling-rounded numbers.\"\nauthors = [\n    { name = \"Your Name\", email = \"your.email@example.com\" },\n]\nlicense = \"MIT\"\nreadme = \"README.md\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.8\"\nkeywords = [\"math\", \"utility\", \"sum-of-squares\", \"ceiling\"]\nclassifiers = [\n    \"Programming Language :: Python :: 3\",\n    \"License :: OSI Approved :: MIT License\",\n    \"Operating System :: OS Independent\",\n    \"Topic :: Scientific/Engineering :: Mathematics\",\n]\n\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"setuptools>=61.0\"]\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n```\n```markdown\n# README.md\n# Sum Squares Calculator\n\nThis project provides a Python utility to calculate the sum of squares of numbers from a given list, after applying a ceiling operation to each number.\n\n## Features\n\n-   Accepts a list of integers or floats.\n-   Applies the ceiling function to each number.\n-   Squares each rounded number.\n-   Sums all the squared numbers.\n\n## Installation\n\nThis is a Python package. You can install it using pip:\n\n```bash\npip install sum-squares-calculator\n```\n\n(Note: For local development, you might install it in editable mode: `pip install -e .`)\n\n## Usage\n\nHere's how to use the `sum_squares` utility function:\n\n```python\nfrom src.calculator import sum_squares\n\n# Example 1: Positive integers\nnumbers1 = [1, 2, 3]\nresult1 = sum_squares(numbers1)\nprint(f\"Sum of squares for {numbers1}: {result1}\") # Expected: 14 (1^2 + 2^2 + 3^2)\n\n# Example 2: Mixed floats and integers\nnumbers2 = [1.4, 4.2, 0]\nresult2 = sum_squares(numbers2)\nprint(f\"Sum of squares for {numbers2}: {result2}\") # Expected: 29 (ceil(1.4)^2 + ceil(4.2)^2 + ceil(0)^2 = 2^2 + 5^2 + 0^2)\n\n# Example 3: Negative numbers\nnumbers3 = [-2.4, 1, 1]\nresult3 = sum_squares(numbers3)\nprint(f\"Sum of squares for {numbers3}: {result3}\") # Expected: 6 (ceil(-2.4)^2 + 1^2 + 1^2 = (-2)^2 + 1^2 + 1^2)\n\n# Example 4: Empty list\nnumbers4 = []\nresult4 = sum_squares(numbers4)\nprint(f\"Sum of squares for {numbers4}: {result4}\") # Expected: 0\n\n# Example 5: Single element\nnumbers5 = [2.1]\nresult5 = sum_squares(numbers5)\nprint(f\"Sum of squares for {numbers5}: {result5}\") # Expected: 9 (ceil(2.1)^2 = 3^2)\n\n# Error handling\ntry:\n    sum_squares([1, \"a\", 3])\nexcept TypeError as e:\n    print(f\"Error caught: {e}\")\n\ntry:\n    sum_squares(\"not a list\")\nexcept TypeError as e:\n    print(f\"Error caught: {e}\")\n```\n\n## Development\n\nTo set up the development environment:\n\n1.  Clone the repository:\n    ```bash\n    git clone https://github.com/your-username/sum-squares-calculator.git\n    cd sum-squares-calculator\n    ```\n2.  Install dependencies:\n    ```bash\n    pip install -e .\n    ```\n\n## Contributing\n\nFeel free to open issues or submit pull requests.\n\n## License\n\nThis project is licensed under the MIT License.\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/133", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\nclass SumSquaresCalculator:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of squares for a list of numbers,\n    rounding each number up to the nearest integer first.\n    \"\"\"\n    def sum_squares(self, lst: list[float | int]) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Computes the sum of squares of numbers from the input list after ceiling-rounding them.\n        Raises TypeError for invalid input list or non-numeric elements.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(lst, list):\n            raise TypeError(\"Input must be a list.\")\n        \n        total_sum = 0\n        for item in lst:\n            if not isinstance(item, (int, float)):\n                raise TypeError(\"List elements must be numbers (int or float).\")\n            \n            rounded_up = math.ceil(item)\n            total_sum += rounded_up * rounded_up\n        return total_sum\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/134", "solution": "```python\nclass StringChecker:\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides utility methods for analyzing string properties.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def check_if_last_char_is_a_letter(input_string: str) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Determines if the last character is an isolated alphabetical character,\n        meaning it's alphabetical and immediately preceded by a space.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not input_string:\n            return False\n\n        last_char = input_string[-1]\n\n        if not last_char.isalpha():\n            return False\n\n        if len(input_string) < 2:\n            return False\n\n        second_to_last_char = input_string[-2]\n\n        if second_to_last_char != ' ':\n            return False\n\n        return True\n```"}
