{"task_id": "HumanEval/140", "solution": "```python\nimport re\n\nclass SpaceFixer:\n    @staticmethod\n    def fix_spaces(text: str) -> str:\n        \"\"\"\n        Replaces single spaces with underscores and sequences of 3+ spaces with a hyphen.\n        Input must be a string, otherwise a TypeError is raised.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(text, str):\n            raise TypeError(\"Input must be a string.\")\n\n        # Replace 3 or more consecutive spaces with a hyphen first.\n        processed_text = re.sub(r' {3,}', '-', text)\n        \n        # Then, replace any remaining single spaces (including those from original '  ' sequences) with an underscore.\n        processed_text = processed_text.replace(' ', '_')\n        \n        return processed_text\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/140", "solution": "```python\n# src/string_formatter/constants.py\nclass StringConstants:\n    \"\"\"\n    Holds constant character values used throughout the string formatting module.\n    This prevents magic strings and improves maintainability.\n    \"\"\"\n    SPACE_CHAR = ' '\n    UNDERSCORE_CHAR = '_'\n    HYPHEN_CHAR = '-'\n\n```\n```python\n# src/string_formatter/formatter.py\nimport re\nfrom .constants import StringConstants\n\nclass StringFormatter:\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides static methods for applying various string formatting rules.\n    Specifically designed for space replacement as per business requirements.\n    \"\"\"\n    @staticmethod\n    def fix_spaces(text: str) -> str:\n        \"\"\"\n        Replaces sequences of more than two spaces with a hyphen and single spaces with underscores.\n        This function standardizes string inputs according to predefined rules.\n        \"\"\"\n        # First, replace sequences of three or more consecutive spaces with a hyphen.\n        # This prevents these longer sequences from being partially replaced by underscores later.\n        text = re.sub(r' {3,}', StringConstants.HYPHEN_CHAR, text)\n        \n        # Second, replace any remaining single spaces (including those from original two-space sequences)\n        # with underscores. This addresses the FR1 requirement.\n        text = text.replace(StringConstants.SPACE_CHAR, StringConstants.UNDERSCORE_CHAR)\n        \n        return text\n\n```\n```python\n# src/string_formatter/__init__.py\n\n```\n```toml\n# pyproject.toml\n[project]\nname = \"string-formatter\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"A utility to standardize string formatting by replacing spaces.\"\nauthors = [{ name = \"Your Name\", email = \"your.email@example.com\" }]\nrequires-python = \">=3.8\"\n\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"setuptools>=61.0\"]\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n\n```\n```markdown\n# README.md\n# String Formatter Utility\n\nThis project provides a Python utility function to standardize string formatting. It replaces single spaces with underscores and sequences of three or more consecutive spaces with a hyphen.\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/139", "solution": "```python\n# src/exceptions.py\nclass InvalidInputError(ValueError):\n    \"\"\"Custom exception for invalid input in factorial calculations.\"\"\"\n    pass\n\n# src/factorial_calculator.py\nfrom typing import Dict\nfrom src.exceptions import InvalidInputError\n\nclass FactorialCalculator:\n    \"\"\"Calculates standard and special factorials with memoization.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._memo: Dict[int, int] = {0: 1, 1: 1}\n\n    def factorial(self, n: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Calculates the standard factorial of a non-negative integer n (n!).\n        Raises InvalidInputError if n is negative or not an integer.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(n, int):\n            raise InvalidInputError(\"Input must be an integer.\")\n        if n < 0:\n            raise InvalidInputError(\"Factorial is not defined for negative numbers.\")\n        \n        if n in self._memo:\n            return self._memo[n]\n        \n        result = 1\n        for i in range(2, n + 1):\n            result *= i\n        self._memo[n] = result\n        return result\n\n    def special_factorial(self, n: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Calculates the special factorial of a positive integer n.\n        Raises InvalidInputError if n is not a positive integer.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(n, int):\n            raise InvalidInputError(\"Input must be an integer.\")\n        if n <= 0:\n            raise InvalidInputError(\"Special factorial is defined only for positive integers (n > 0).\")\n        \n        product = 1\n        for i in range(1, n + 1):\n            product *= self.factorial(i)\n        return product\n\n# pyproject.toml\n[project]\nname = \"special-factorial-calculator\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"A Python library to calculate standard and special factorials.\"\nreadme = \"README.md\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.8\"\nlicense = { text = \"MIT\" }\nkeywords = [\"factorial\", \"special factorial\", \"brazilian factorial\", \"mathematics\"]\nauthors = [\n    { name = \"Your Name\", email = \"your.email@example.com\" }\n]\nclassifiers = [\n    \"Development Status :: 3 - Alpha\",\n    \"Intended Audience :: Developers\",\n    \"License :: OSI Approved :: MIT License\",\n    \"Programming Language :: Python :: 3\",\n    \"Programming Language :: Python :: 3.8\",\n    \"Programming Language :: Python :: 3.9\",\n    \"Programming Language :: Python :: 3.10\",\n    \"Programming Language :: Python :: 3.11\",\n    \"Programming Language :: Python :: 3.12\",\n    \"Operating System :: OS Independent\",\n    \"Topic :: Scientific/Engineering :: Mathematics\",\n    \"Topic :: Software Development :: Libraries :: Python Modules\"\n]\n\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"setuptools>=61.0\"]\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n\n[tool.setuptools.packages.find]\nwhere = [\"src\"]\n\n# README.md\n# Special Factorial Calculator\n\nA Python library designed to accurately calculate standard factorials (`n!`) and \"special factorials\" (also known as Brazilian factorials, `n! * (n-1)! * ... * 1!`) for given positive integer inputs. The library uses memoization to optimize performance for repeated factorial calculations.\n\n## Features\n\n-   **Standard Factorial Calculation**: Compute `n!` for non-negative integers.\n-   **Special Factorial Calculation**: Compute `n! * (n-1)! * ... * 1!` for positive integers.\n-   **Memoization**: Efficiently caches previously computed factorial values to speed up subsequent calculations.\n-   **Robust Error Handling**: Raises `InvalidInputError` for invalid inputs (e.g., negative numbers, non-integers).\n\n## Installation\n\nThis project is intended to be used as a Python library. You can clone the repository and use it directly.\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/your-username/special-factorial-calculator.git\ncd special-factorial-calculator\n```\n\n## Usage\n\nHere's how to use the `FactorialCalculator` class:\n\n```python\nfrom src.factorial_calculator import FactorialCalculator\nfrom src.exceptions import InvalidInputError\n\n# Create an instance of the calculator\ncalculator = FactorialCalculator()\n\n# Calculate standard factorial\ntry:\n    result_5_factorial = calculator.factorial(5)\n    print(f\"5! = {result_5_factorial}\") # Output: 5! = 120\n\n    result_0_factorial = calculator.factorial(0)\n    print(f\"0! = {result_0_factorial}\") # Output: 0! = 1\nexcept InvalidInputError as e:\n    print(f\"Error: {e}\")\n\n# Calculate special factorial\ntry:\n    result_3_special = calculator.special_factorial(3)\n    print(f\"Special factorial of 3 = {result_3_special}\") # Output: Special factorial of 3 = 12 (3! * 2! * 1! = 6 * 2 * 1)\n\n    result_4_special = calculator.special_factorial(4)\n    print(f\"Special factorial of 4 = {result_4_special}\") # Output: Special factorial of 4 = 288 (4! * 3! * 2! * 1! = 24 * 6 * 2 * 1)\nexcept InvalidInputError as e:\n    print(f\"Error: {e}\")\n\n# Example of invalid input handling\ntry:\n    calculator.factorial(-1)\nexcept InvalidInputError as e:\n    print(f\"Expected error for factorial(-1): {e}\")\n\ntry:\n    calculator.special_factorial(0)\nexcept InvalidInputError as e:\n    print(f\"Expected error for special_factorial(0): {e}\")\n\ntry:\n    calculator.factorial(3.5)\nexcept InvalidInputError as e:\n    print(f\"Expected error for factorial(3.5): {e}\")\n```\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 src/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 __init__.py\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 factorial_calculator.py\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 exceptions.py\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 pyproject.toml\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 README.md\n```\n\n## License\n\nThis project is licensed under the MIT License.\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/142", "solution": "# src/models.py\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\n@dataclass\nclass IntegerList:\n    values: list[int] = field(default_factory=list)\n\n# src/calculator.py\nfrom typing import List\n\nclass ListProcessor:\n    def process_list(self, lst: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n        processed_lst = list(lst)  # Operate on a copy to preserve the original input list.\n        for i in range(len(processed_lst)):\n            if i % 3 == 0:  # Rule for multiples of 3 (including 0) takes precedence.\n                processed_lst[i] = processed_lst[i] ** 2\n            elif i % 4 == 0:  # Rule for multiples of 4 (including 0), only if not a multiple of 3.\n                processed_lst[i] = processed_lst[i] ** 3\n        return processed_lst\n\n    def calculate_sum(self, lst: List[int]) -> int:\n        return sum(lst)\n\ndef sum_squares(lst: List[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    This function processes a list of integers, transforming elements based on their index.\n    It returns the sum of the transformed elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not lst:\n        return 0\n\n    processor = ListProcessor()\n    modified_list = processor.process_list(lst)\n    total_sum = processor.calculate_sum(modified_list)\n    return total_sum"}
