{"task_id": "HumanEval/145", "solution": "def _sum_digits(num: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of the absolute digits of an integer.\n    For example: _sum_digits(-12) == 3 (1+2).\n    \"\"\"\n    s = 0\n    num_abs = abs(num)\n    while num_abs > 0:\n        s += num_abs % 10\n        num_abs //= 10\n    return s\n\ndef order_by_points(nums: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sorts the given list of integers by the sum of their digits in ascending order.\n    For ties, elements are ordered by their original index in the input list.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not nums:\n        return []\n\n    decorated_list = []\n    for index, value in enumerate(nums):\n        digit_sum = _sum_digits(value)\n        decorated_list.append((digit_sum, index, value))\n\n    decorated_list.sort()\n\n    return [item[2] for item in decorated_list]"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/144", "solution": "```python\nclass Fraction:\n    \"\"\"\n    Represents a mathematical fraction with a numerator and a denominator.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, numerator: int, denominator: int):\n        if not isinstance(numerator, int) or not isinstance(denominator, int):\n            raise TypeError(\"Numerator and denominator must be integers.\")\n        if denominator == 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Denominator cannot be zero.\")\n        if numerator <= 0 or denominator <= 0: # This constraint is stricter than the original problem for positive whole numbers.\n            raise ValueError(\"Numerator and denominator must be positive.\")\n\n        self.numerator = numerator\n        self.denominator = denominator\n\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        if not isinstance(other, Fraction):\n            return NotImplemented\n        return self.numerator == other.numerator and self.denominator == other.denominator\n\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return f\"Fraction({self.numerator}/{self.denominator})\"\n\nclass FractionParser:\n    \"\"\"\n    Utility class for parsing fraction strings into Fraction objects.\n    \"\"\"\n    @staticmethod\n    def parse(fraction_str: str) -> Fraction:\n        \"\"\"\n        Parses a fraction string (e.g., \"3/4\") into a Fraction object.\n        \"\"\"\n        parts = fraction_str.split('/')\n        if len(parts) != 2:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Invalid fraction string format: {fraction_str}. Expected 'numerator/denominator'.\")\n        \n        try:\n            numerator = int(parts[0])\n            denominator = int(parts[1])\n        except ValueError:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Invalid number in fraction string: {fraction_str}. Numerator and denominator must be integers.\")\n\n        return Fraction(numerator, denominator)\n\ndef simplify(x: str, n: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Your task is to implement a function that will simplify the expression\n    x * n. The function returns True if x * n evaluates to a whole number and False\n    otherwise. Both x and n, are string representation of a fraction, and have the following format,\n    <numerator>/<denominator> where both numerator and denominator are positive whole numbers.\n\n    You can assume that x, and n are valid fractions, and do not have zero as denominator.\n\n    simplify(\"1/5\", \"5/1\") = True\n    simplify(\"1/6\", \"2/1\") = False\n    simplify(\"7/10\", \"10/2\") = False\n    \"\"\"\n    fraction_x = FractionParser.parse(x)\n    fraction_n = FractionParser.parse(n)\n\n    product_numerator = fraction_x.numerator * fraction_n.numerator\n    product_denominator = fraction_x.denominator * fraction_n.denominator\n\n    # Check if the product is a whole number\n    return product_numerator % product_denominator == 0\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/145", "solution": "```python\n# src/utils.py\nclass DigitSumCalculator:\n    def calculate_digit_sum(self, num: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Calculates the sum of the absolute value of an integer's digits.\n        Handles negative numbers and zero by taking the absolute value.\"\"\"\n        s = 0\n        num_str = str(abs(num))\n        for digit_char in num_str:\n            s += int(digit_char)\n        return s\n\n```\n```python\n# src/sorter.py\nfrom src.utils import DigitSumCalculator\n\nclass OrderByPointsSorter:\n    def __init__(self, digit_sum_calculator: DigitSumCalculator):\n        \"\"\"Initializes the sorter with a DigitSumCalculator instance.\"\"\"\n        self._digit_sum_calculator = digit_sum_calculator\n\n    def order_by_points(self, nums: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n        \"\"\"Sorts integers by sum of digits, then by original index for ties.\n        Returns a new list with the sorted integers.\"\"\"\n        if not nums:\n            return []\n\n        decorated_list = []\n        for index, num in enumerate(nums):\n            sum_digits = self._digit_sum_calculator.calculate_digit_sum(num)\n            decorated_list.append((sum_digits, index, num))\n\n        decorated_list.sort()\n\n        sorted_nums = [item[2] for item in decorated_list]\n        return sorted_nums\n\n_digit_sum_calculator_instance = DigitSumCalculator()\ndef order_by_points(nums: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"Convenience function to use the OrderByPointsSorter.\"\"\"\n    sorter = OrderByPointsSorter(_digit_sum_calculator_instance)\n    return sorter.order_by_points(nums)\n\n```\n```toml\n# pyproject.toml\n[project]\nname = \"custom_integer_sorter\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"A utility to sort a list of integers based on a custom criterion.\"\nauthors = [\n    { name = \"Your Name\", email = \"your.email@example.com\" },\n]\nrequires-python = \">=3.8\"\nlicense = { text = \"MIT\" }\n\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"setuptools>=61.0\"]\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/146", "solution": "```python\n# src/special_filter/__init__.py\n# This file marks the special_filter directory as a Python package.\n```\n```python\n# src/special_filter/utils.py\n\nclass NumberUtils:\n    \"\"\"\n    NumberUtils provides static utility methods for extracting digits and checking their parity.\n    It handles absolute values for digit extraction.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def is_odd(digit: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Checks if a given digit is odd.\n        Odd digits are defined as 1, 3, 5, 7, 9.\n        \"\"\"\n        return digit in (1, 3, 5, 7, 9)\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def get_first_digit(num: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Extracts the first (most significant) digit of an integer.\n        Handles negative numbers by taking the absolute value.\n        \"\"\"\n        abs_num = abs(num)\n        if abs_num == 0:\n            return 0\n        while abs_num >= 10:\n            abs_num //= 10\n        return abs_num\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def get_last_digit(num: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Extracts the last (least significant) digit of an integer.\n        Handles negative numbers by taking the absolute value.\n        \"\"\"\n        return abs(num) % 10\n```\n```python\n# src/special_filter/filter_service.py\n\nfrom .utils import NumberUtils\n\nclass FilterService:\n    \"\"\"\n    FilterService orchestrates the filtering process.\n    It iterates through numbers and applies specified criteria using helper utilities.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def special_filter(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Takes a list of integers and returns the count of numbers that are greater than 10\n        and have both their first and last digits as odd.\n        \"\"\"\n        count = 0\n        for num in nums:\n            # FR2: Identify numbers greater than 10.\n            if num > 10:\n                first_digit = NumberUtils.get_first_digit(num)\n                last_digit = NumberUtils.get_last_digit(num)\n                \n                # FR3: Identify numbers where both the first and last digits are odd.\n                if NumberUtils.is_odd(first_digit) and NumberUtils.is_odd(last_digit):\n                    count += 1 # FR4: Count satisfying elements.\n        return count\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/146", "solution": "```python\n# src/special_filter/__init__.py\n\"\"\"Initializes the special_filter package.\"\"\"\n```\n\n```python\n# src/special_filter/filter_service.py\nfrom typing import List\nfrom .utils import DigitUtils\n\nclass FilterService:\n    @staticmethod\n    def specialFilter(nums: List[int]) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Filters an array of numbers and returns the count of elements that are\n        greater than 10 and whose first and last digits are odd.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(nums, list):\n            raise TypeError(\"Input must be a list.\")\n        \n        count = 0\n        for num in nums:\n            if not isinstance(num, int):\n                raise ValueError(f\"List elements must be integers, got {type(num)}.\")\n            \n            # Check FR2: number greater than 10\n            if num > 10:\n                # Check FR3: first and last digits are odd\n                first_digit = DigitUtils.get_first_digit(num)\n                last_digit = DigitUtils.get_last_digit(num)\n                \n                if DigitUtils.is_odd(first_digit) and DigitUtils.is_odd(last_digit):\n                    count += 1 # FR4: increment count if both criteria met\n        return count # FR5: return total count\n```\n\n```python\n# src/special_filter/utils.py\nclass DigitUtils:\n    @staticmethod\n    def is_odd(digit: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if a given digit is odd (1, 3, 5, 7, 9).\"\"\"\n        return digit in (1, 3, 5, 7, 9)\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def get_first_digit(num: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Extracts the first digit of an integer, using its absolute value.\"\"\"\n        abs_num = abs(num)\n        if abs_num == 0:\n            return 0\n        while abs_num >= 10:\n            abs_num //= 10\n        return abs_num\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def get_last_digit(num: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Extracts the last digit of an integer, using its absolute value.\"\"\"\n        return abs(num) % 10\n```\n\n```toml\n# pyproject.toml\n[project]\nname = \"special-filter\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"A system to identify and count numbers meeting specific criteria.\"\nauthors = [\n    { name = \"Your Name\", email = \"your.email@example.com\" }\n]\nrequires-python = \">=3.8\"\ndependencies = []\n\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"setuptools>=61.0\"]\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n\n[tool.pytest.ini_options]\npythonpath = \"src\"\n```\n\n```markdown\n# README.md\n# Special Number Filter\n\nThis project implements a Python function to filter a list of numbers based on specific numerical and digit-based criteria.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Python 3.8+\n\n## Installation\n\n1. Clone the repository:\n   ```bash\n   git clone <repository-url>\n   cd special-filter\n   ```\n2. Install the project in editable mode:\n   ```bash\n   pip install -e .\n   ```\n\n## Usage\n\nThe primary functionality is provided by the `specialFilter` function within the `FilterService` class.\n\n```python\nfrom special_filter.filter_service import FilterService\n\n# Example usage:\nnumbers_list = [15, -73, 14, -15, 33, 21, 109, 11, 13, 101, 213, 9]\ncount = FilterService.specialFilter(numbers_list)\nprint(f\"Number of special numbers: {count}\")\n# Expected output: Number of special numbers: 7\n# (15, 33, 21, 109, 11, 13, 101 are special based on criteria)\n# (213 is not special because its first digit is 2, which is even)\n```\n\n### Criteria for \"Special Numbers\"\n\nA number is considered \"special\" if:\n1. It is greater than 10.\n2. Its first digit is odd (1, 3, 5, 7, 9).\n3. Its last digit is odd (1, 3, 5, 7, 9).\n\nNegative numbers are handled by considering their absolute value for digit extraction, but they will never satisfy the \"greater than 10\" condition.\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 src/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 special_filter/\n\u2502   \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 __init__.py\n\u2502   \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 filter_service.py\n\u2502   \u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 utils.py\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 pyproject.toml\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 README.md\n```"}
