{"task_id": "HumanEval/70", "solution": "```\nsrc/strange_sort/__init__.py\n```\n\n```\nsrc/strange_sort/sorter.py\n```python\nfrom typing import List\n\nclass StrangeSorter:\n    \"\"\"\n    Encapsulates the core logic for performing the \"strange sort\".\n    This class is designed to be stateless, operating purely on the input provided.\n    \"\"\"\n    def sort(self, input_list: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Sorts a list of integers in a strange order (min, max, min, max...).\n        It returns a new list without modifying the original input.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not input_list:\n            return []\n\n        temp_list = sorted(input_list)\n        result = []\n        left, right = 0, len(temp_list) - 1\n        is_min_turn = True\n\n        while left <= right:\n            if is_min_turn:\n                result.append(temp_list[left])\n                left += 1\n            else:\n                result.append(temp_list[right])\n                right -= 1\n            is_min_turn = not is_min_turn\n        return result\n\ndef strange_sort_list(lst: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides the public entry point for the strange sorting functionality.\n    It delegates the actual sorting task to a StrangeSorter instance.\n    \"\"\"\n    sorter = StrangeSorter()\n    return sorter.sort(lst)\n\n```\n```\nsrc/main.py\n```python\nfrom strange_sort.sorter import strange_sort_list\n\ndef main() -> None:\n    \"\"\"\n    Example usage of the strange_sort_list function.\n    Demonstrates how to call the function with various inputs.\n    \"\"\"\n    test_cases = [\n        [],\n        [5],\n        [1, 2, 3, 4],\n        [1, 2, 3, 4, 5],\n        [5, 5, 5, 5],\n        [1, 2, 1, 3, 2],\n        [10, 20, 30, 40],\n        [40, 30, 20, 10],\n        [-1, -5, 0, 2],\n        [-10, -20, -30],\n    ]\n\n    print(\"Strange Sort List Examples:\")\n    for i, lst in enumerate(test_cases):\n        sorted_lst = strange_sort_list(lst)\n        print(f\"Test {i+1}: Input: {lst}, Output: {sorted_lst}\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n\n```\n```\npyproject.toml\n```toml\n[project]\nname = \"strange-sort\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"A utility to sort a list of integers in a specific 'strange' order.\"\nauthors = [\n    { name = \"Your Name\", email = \"your.email@example.com\" },\n]\ndependencies = []\nreadme = \"README.md\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.8\"\nlicense = { text = \"MIT\" }\n\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"setuptools>=61.0\"]\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n\n[project.urls]\nHomepage = \"https://github.com/yourusername/strange-sort\" # Replace with your repo URL\n\n```\n```\nREADME.md\n```markdown\n# Strange Sort List Utility\n\nThis project provides a Python utility to sort a list of integers in a unique \"strange\" order. The sorting algorithm alternately picks the minimum and maximum remaining elements from the input list.\n\n## Features\n\n-   Sorts lists of integers in a min-max-min-max fashion.\n-   Handles empty lists and lists with duplicate elements.\n-   Provides a clean, functional interface.\n\n## Installation\n\nYou can install this package using pip (once published to PyPI or from source):\n\n```bash\n# From PyPI (if published)\npip install strange-sort\n\n# From local source\npip install .\n```\n\n## Usage\n\nHere's how to use the `strange_sort_list` function:\n\n```python\nfrom strange_sort.sorter import strange_sort_list\n\n# Example 1: Basic list\nmy_list_1 = [1, 2, 3, 4]\nsorted_list_1 = strange_sort_list(my_list_1)\nprint(f\"Input: {my_list_1}, Sorted: {sorted_list_1}\")\n# Expected output: Input: [1, 2, 3, 4], Sorted: [1, 4, 2, 3]\n\n# Example 2: List with odd number of elements\nmy_list_2 = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nsorted_list_2 = strange_sort_list(my_list_2)\nprint(f\"Input: {my_list_2}, Sorted: {sorted_list_2}\")\n# Expected output: Input: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5], Sorted: [1, 5, 2, 4, 3]\n\n# Example 3: List with duplicates\nmy_list_3 = [5, 5, 5, 5]\nsorted_list_3 = strange_sort_list(my_list_3)\nprint(f\"Input: {my_list_3}, Sorted: {sorted_list_3}\")\n# Expected output: Input: [5, 5, 5, 5], Sorted: [5, 5, 5, 5]\n\n# Example 4: Empty list\nmy_list_4 = []\nsorted_list_4 = strange_sort_list(my_list_4)\nprint(f\"Input: {my_list_4}, Sorted: {sorted_list_4}\")\n# Expected output: Input: [], Sorted: []\n```\n\nYou can also run the `main.py` script for more examples:\n\n```bash\npython src/main.py\n```\n\n## Development\n\nTo set up the development environment:\n\n1.  Clone the repository:\n    ```bash\n    git clone https://github.com/yourusername/strange-sort.git\n    cd strange-sort\n    ```\n2.  Install dependencies:\n    ```bash\n    pip install -e .\n    ```\n\n## Testing\n\nUnit tests are provided using `pytest`.\n*(Note: The `tests/test_sorter.py` file is not included in this generation as per instructions, but is part of the overall design.)*\n\nTo run the tests:\n\n```bash\npytest tests/\n```\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```\n.\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 src/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 strange_sort/\n\u2502   \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 __init__.py      # Package initializer\n\u2502   \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 sorter.py        # Contains StrangeSorter class and strange_sort_list function\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 main.py              # Example usage script\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 tests/\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 test_sorter.py       # Unit tests (not included in generated code output)\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 pyproject.toml           # Project metadata and dependencies\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 README.md                # Project description and usage instructions\n```\n\n## License\n\nThis project is licensed under the MIT License.\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/95", "solution": "```python\n\"\"\"Package for dictionary key casing validation.\"\"\"\n\nclass DictCaseChecker:\n    \"\"\"Encapsulates the core logic for determining dictionary key casing consistency.\"\"\"\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def check_dict_case(data: dict) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Validates if dictionary keys are consistently cased.\n        Returns True if all keys are strings and either all lowercase or all uppercase, False otherwise.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not data:\n            return False\n\n        all_keys = list(data.keys())\n        \n        # Check if all keys are strings\n        if not all(isinstance(key, str) for key in all_keys):\n            return False\n\n        # Check if all keys are lowercase\n        all_lower = all(key.islower() for key in all_keys)\n        if all_lower:\n            return True\n\n        # Check if all keys are uppercase\n        all_upper = all(key.isupper() for key in all_keys)\n        if all_upper:\n            return True\n\n        return False\n\nclass DictCaseCheckerService:\n    \"\"\"Provides the public interface for dictionary key casing validation.\"\"\"\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def check_dict_case(data: dict) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Provides a service interface for dictionary key casing validation.\n        Delegates the core logic to DictCaseChecker.\n        \"\"\"\n        return DictCaseChecker.check_dict_case(data)\n```\n"}
